-  opening:"Public Act 103-0491 opened a new municipal delivery path. It did not create projects, funding, adoption, or guaranteed competitive access. The useful question is therefore not simply whether an RFP exists, but whether independent public evidence shows an eligible owner moving from a consequential need toward a funded project while the delivery path remains genuinely open.",
+  opening:"Public Act 103-0491 opened a new municipal delivery path. Public Act 104-0395 later amended the municipal selection procedure, effective July 1, 2025, including a discretionary single-response path. Neither Act created projects, funding, adoption, or guaranteed competitive access. The useful question is therefore not simply whether an RFP exists, but whether independent public evidence shows an eligible owner moving from a consequential need toward a funded project while the delivery path remains genuinely open.",
   executive_sections:[
     {label:"WHAT CHANGED",title:"A municipality may buy design and construction together.",body:"Illinois municipalities may elect a design-build contract that places design and construction responsibility in one entity. If they elect it, the Act imposes advance notice, project-definition, qualifications, technical/cost evaluation, conflict, award, and reporting rules. Authorization is statutory fact. Adoption and commercial demand remain evidence questions."},
     {label:"WHY IT MATTERS",title:"The law changes the set of possible delivery choices.",body:"An integrated firm can now compete under a municipal process that expressly values personnel, similar experience, financial capacity, performance, technical quality, innovation, constructability, cost, and time. Price is excluded from Phase I, and total-project-cost weighting may not exceed 30 percent in Phase II. That makes demonstrated integration potentially relevant; it does not prove qualification or competitive advantage."},
-    {label:"WHO IS COVERED",title:"Municipalities—and, under a separate article, school districts.",body:"The Act creates parallel authority for Illinois municipalities and school districts. This municipal lens must not be silently extended to counties, park districts, state agencies, or other public bodies. School rules also contain differences, including the exact $12 million boundary and a single-respondent provision."},
+    {label:"WHO IS COVERED",title:"Municipalities—and, under a separate article, school districts.",body:"The foundation Act creates legally separate authority for Illinois municipalities and school districts. This municipal lens must not be silently extended to counties, park districts, state agencies, or other public bodies. The exact $12 million boundary remains one difference between the municipal and school pathways. A single-response option is no longer a school-only distinction: current municipal law also permits it, subject to municipal discretion and a best-interest finding."},
+    {label:"CURRENT MUNICIPAL SELECTION RULE",title:"Two to six ordinarily; one only through the statutory exception.",body:"The ordinary municipal Phase II shortlist remains two to six design-build entities. Under P.A. 104-0395, if the municipality receives only one Phase I response, it may proceed with a Phase II evaluation of that respondent if the municipality, in its discretion, finds proceeding to be in its best interest. One response creates permission to proceed, not a duty to do so."},
     {label:"THE PUBLIC-RECORD FOOTPRINT",title:"The RFP is late evidence of earlier decisions.",body:"By RFP issuance, an owner must be able to state schedule, budget, funding source, available funds, qualifications, performance criteria, evaluation criteria, and Phase II field size. Earlier traces may appear in agendas, minutes, capital plans, budgets, facility assessments, master plans, professional-services selections, ordinances, financing records, bond material, grants, and project studies. Their relevance must be established document by document."},
     {label:"WHEN TO INVESTIGATE",title:"Look for convergence, then ask what is still missing.",body:"Serious attention becomes more defensible when an eligible owner, documented need, governing-body attention, planning activity, and plausible funding path converge while delivery-method choice remains unresolved. The next question should target the weakest link: Is the project approved? Is money available? Who is advising the owner? Has delivery already been selected? Could prior involvement restrict later eligibility?"},
